WEBVTT

00:06.673 --> 00:08.800
We've finally arrived…

00:12.137 --> 00:14.764
in America!

00:23.398 --> 00:25.859
It's been a long time coming.

00:26.443 --> 00:28.236
At long last,

00:28.319 --> 00:32.657
-land!
-Meat!

00:36.995 --> 00:38.329
In fact,

00:38.413 --> 00:41.332
we did not get to eat a lot of meat
throughout our trip.

00:45.003 --> 00:47.630
Yes, especially the battle team

00:47.714 --> 00:52.343
might need animal protein
to train properly.

00:54.637 --> 00:56.139
I see!

00:56.222 --> 00:59.434
Maybe we can hunt some animals
once we get off.

00:59.517 --> 01:01.770
I wonder what we can find in America.

01:01.853 --> 01:04.397
I don't know
since it's my first trip abroad.

01:05.899 --> 01:09.069
I never thought things
would turn out like this for me.

01:09.152 --> 01:10.612
I wouldn't call this a trip.

01:12.489 --> 01:16.201
Seeing that big oaf so excited,
it must be a good sign.

01:20.538 --> 01:22.916
Look, beyond the fog…

02:00.245 --> 02:03.623
Well, we all knew that, didn't we?

02:03.706 --> 02:07.961
But, it's our first time
abroad in this new world,

02:08.044 --> 02:11.422
so we had no idea what happened here.

02:12.882 --> 02:14.008
<i>I really hoped</i>

02:14.092 --> 02:16.803
<i>that the story I made up</i>
<i>as fake Lillian were true,</i>

02:16.886 --> 02:19.806
<i>that only Japan got petrified</i>

02:19.889 --> 02:23.017
<i>while the rest of the world</i>
<i>got rebuilt long ago.</i>

02:23.101 --> 02:24.060
<i>Or rather,</i>

02:24.144 --> 02:27.981
<i>maybe they managed to defeat</i>
<i>the petrification beam from the start.</i>

02:28.731 --> 02:32.068
<i>Deep down I thought that maybe…</i>

02:32.152 --> 02:33.403
<i>Just maybe!</i>

02:34.571 --> 02:35.905
<i>That's what I thought.</i>

02:47.500 --> 02:48.543
That cape over there…

02:49.627 --> 02:52.380
I could see a huge clutter of statues.

02:53.506 --> 02:54.465
You're right.

02:55.466 --> 02:56.467
Why?

02:57.927 --> 02:59.929
I could see a huge car wreck.

03:01.639 --> 03:03.975
<i>The vehicles got all completely rusted,</i>

03:04.058 --> 03:06.603
<i>leaving nothing but statues stuck inside.</i>

03:08.146 --> 03:11.024
But this is the sea,
how did the cars end up here?

03:13.234 --> 03:14.944
This used to be a road full of vehicles.

03:16.905 --> 03:19.324
<i>I used to sail my ship down here</i>

03:20.158 --> 03:21.868
<i>thousands of years ago.</i>

03:22.827 --> 03:23.953
<i>Right here,</i>

03:24.037 --> 03:27.206
<i>there once stood the gateway</i>
<i>to the Pacific Ocean.</i>

03:27.707 --> 03:31.336
<i>In the midst of San Francisco's mist,</i>
<i>there was the splendid, radiant, crimson</i>

03:31.419 --> 03:33.546
<i>Golden Gate Bridge.</i>

03:36.049 --> 03:39.594
All of them are gone for real,
aren't they?

03:39.677 --> 03:41.888
All the things that people made…

03:41.971 --> 03:43.890
Deep down inside,

03:43.973 --> 03:47.602
I have a sliver of hope
that there'd be someone to help us.

03:49.312 --> 03:52.023
No, that's not the way, guys!

03:53.066 --> 03:55.360
We just need to gather and harvest corn

03:55.443 --> 03:58.988
to bring every statue back to life

03:59.072 --> 04:00.782
with the power of science!

04:01.616 --> 04:02.450
That's right.

04:03.910 --> 04:06.663
We're going to save

04:07.622 --> 04:09.165
the whole of humanity.

04:09.249 --> 04:11.709
All 7.8 billion of them.

04:13.002 --> 04:18.466
ORIGINAL WORK: RIICHIRO INAGAKI, BOICHI
SHUEISHA <i>JUMP COMICS</i>

05:53.936 --> 05:57.065
Finally, we're getting off

05:57.148 --> 05:59.233
in America!

06:02.487 --> 06:05.823
This is Lillian's native country…

06:07.366 --> 06:09.368
So, is this America?

06:09.452 --> 06:12.497
I could only imagine
what this place would be like

06:12.580 --> 06:14.082
from what everyone says…

06:15.333 --> 06:17.960
It's not too different from Japan…

06:19.462 --> 06:21.214
Isn't this your average forest?

06:25.718 --> 06:28.805
Every American-ish landmark
has disappeared.

06:28.888 --> 06:30.348
It's not surprising at all…

06:33.768 --> 06:35.353
A pine cone!

06:37.188 --> 06:39.774
It is huge!

06:39.857 --> 06:42.360
Yes, this is a Sequoia.

06:43.069 --> 06:44.612
"Sequoia"?

06:46.155 --> 06:47.198
This one.

06:48.407 --> 06:51.202
No way! It's abnormally big!

06:52.328 --> 06:55.540
I've spent quite a long time in forests,

06:55.623 --> 06:58.709
but I'd never seen
such a magnificent tree!

06:59.293 --> 07:01.379
As you can see,

07:01.462 --> 07:03.047
Sequoias are over 80 meters tall.

07:03.131 --> 07:06.050
They're ridiculously huge,
the tallest tree on Earth.

07:06.134 --> 07:09.220
What? 80 meters?

07:15.893 --> 07:20.314
The world has fared so well,
even after civilization, huh?

07:22.400 --> 07:25.069
America is awesome…

07:29.991 --> 07:32.368
We'll split into two teams here
at the Sacramento River.

07:33.077 --> 07:35.288
The resource gathering team
will remain by the ship,

07:35.955 --> 07:38.916
will the corn search party
will move further inland.

07:39.667 --> 07:43.129
Those who can fight
will guard the search party,

07:43.212 --> 07:45.423
who may get attacked by wild animals.

08:01.689 --> 08:04.233
We're going to look for corn everywhere,

08:04.317 --> 08:05.693
until our legs hurt so bad!

08:05.776 --> 08:08.613
I think that's impossible…

08:08.696 --> 08:10.698
America is unusually vast.

08:10.781 --> 08:14.368
Whether America's
iconic corn has survived,

08:14.452 --> 08:17.955
even if it's just a hybrid kind,
is a total gamble in itself.

08:18.789 --> 08:20.500
If there's still a corn belt,

08:20.583 --> 08:23.085
then it should be right around here.

08:24.378 --> 08:27.673
<i>Since we're on the West Coast,</i>
<i>our destination is Northern California.</i>

08:31.636 --> 08:33.721
We'll get in through San Francisco Bay

08:33.804 --> 08:36.307
and go up the Sacramento River
to the Sacramento City area--

08:36.390 --> 08:38.518
Wait, Senku!

08:38.601 --> 08:42.980
We don't know that much
about American geography.

08:43.731 --> 08:47.652
So we just have to follow this river
to reach the corn, won't we?

08:47.735 --> 08:49.737
Let's go!

08:49.820 --> 08:53.950
It's amazing how you seem
to feel at home, Chrome.

08:54.033 --> 08:56.369
That's my search party leader.

09:01.415 --> 09:03.376
Fall is almost over,

09:03.459 --> 09:06.212
and the corn is about to wither.

09:06.295 --> 09:07.713
I hope we make it on time.

09:08.339 --> 09:10.216
Yes, we should be fine.

09:11.425 --> 09:14.220
<i>The Earth's precession</i>

09:14.303 --> 09:16.931
<i>is like the wobbling tilt</i>
<i>of a spinning top.</i>

09:17.014 --> 09:17.932
EARTH'S PRECESSION

09:18.015 --> 09:19.475
<i>Thankfully,</i>

09:19.559 --> 09:22.103
<i>the slight tilt of the axis</i>
<i>for the past 3,700 years</i>

09:22.186 --> 09:27.191
has thankfully caused a nine-day delay
in the Earth's weather patterns.

09:27.275 --> 09:29.026
-Wow!
-Wow!

09:29.110 --> 09:30.820
Then we're barely on time!

09:31.612 --> 09:32.947
The only uncertainty would be

09:33.030 --> 09:36.993
an unusual shift in the ecosystem
due to climate change.

09:37.076 --> 09:40.454
We'd be grateful
if an animal didn't attack us.

09:41.247 --> 09:44.750
We should be grateful
if an animal did attack us, right?

09:45.710 --> 09:46.544
Yes.

09:49.088 --> 09:51.090
Huh? Isn't it the opposite?

09:59.640 --> 10:02.768
Not again, right after Senku said it!

10:02.852 --> 10:06.272
I knew it, for real…

10:06.355 --> 10:08.357
This is not just bad luck, is it?

10:08.441 --> 10:10.359
Wait, there's no such thing as luck!

10:10.443 --> 10:14.030
It's just Senku and his scientific mind
spitting out the worst-case scenarios

10:14.113 --> 10:16.032
that pop into his head constantly!

10:19.952 --> 10:21.829
It's a bask of American crocodiles!

10:24.415 --> 10:26.834
This is bad, there's so many of them!

10:39.305 --> 10:42.350
-Meat!
-Meat!

10:44.435 --> 10:45.603
Meat!

10:46.270 --> 10:47.521
Meat!

10:48.147 --> 10:52.026
Now I understand
why you wanted to be attacked.

10:52.109 --> 10:54.195
-Meat!
-Meat!

10:54.862 --> 10:58.741
Yes, I'm really grateful
for the cycle of life.

11:01.577 --> 11:03.329
This is impressive.

11:04.955 --> 11:09.293
The strongest all-star team!

11:33.484 --> 11:35.444
This is America!

11:36.654 --> 11:39.448
Now that we've come ashore,
we've got to eat this.

12:01.887 --> 12:03.639
Enjoy them, please.

12:03.722 --> 12:05.724
These are crocodile hamburgers.

12:07.810 --> 12:09.979
-Hamburgers!
-Hamburgers!

12:10.062 --> 12:12.356
Hamburgers?

12:13.023 --> 12:15.860
This is how you eat them.

12:27.496 --> 12:29.790
It's so yummy!

12:32.877 --> 12:35.379
Awesome! It's so good!

12:35.463 --> 12:38.048
What's with this juiciness!

12:38.132 --> 12:40.176
I had never tried anything like this!

13:00.696 --> 13:01.989
Senku.

13:03.282 --> 13:05.034
A crocodile swallowed this whole.

13:07.786 --> 13:09.330
Was there something inside them?

13:20.090 --> 13:21.217
Corn?

13:24.553 --> 13:27.431
Is this the so-called American specialty?

13:28.849 --> 13:30.601
It slid in.

13:31.310 --> 13:35.356
This proves that the seed
to revive all of humanity is upstream.

13:39.652 --> 13:41.862
Is this the corn of life?

13:41.946 --> 13:43.155
Yes.

13:43.239 --> 13:46.283
This is America's iconic cheat code corn,

13:46.367 --> 13:48.744
the great Yellow Dent.

13:48.827 --> 13:50.663
Cheat code corn?

13:51.664 --> 13:53.582
Well, it tastes terrible.

13:53.666 --> 13:57.253
It's just ridiculously efficient
to extract alcohol.

13:58.796 --> 14:02.925
In that case, we're really close
to bring humanity back to life!

14:03.008 --> 14:04.343
Just wait for us!

14:04.426 --> 14:07.888
You're up next, American statues!

14:07.972 --> 14:12.184
We don't know how much of this corn
is still remaining, though.

14:12.268 --> 14:14.186
So what? Even if there were only a few,

14:14.270 --> 14:16.605
we can still harvest and get more of them!

14:16.689 --> 14:19.233
It would eventually turn
into a battle against time.

14:19.733 --> 14:22.486
Before the enemies get to us,

14:22.570 --> 14:26.031
we've got to head there quick,
back to Why-man!

14:26.115 --> 14:28.868
Yes, let's make it right away.

14:28.951 --> 14:30.494
First stop, Corn City!

14:36.458 --> 14:38.127
Those are corn kernels!

14:39.545 --> 14:41.630
They keep coming one after another.

14:43.215 --> 14:45.217
Hyoga…

14:47.136 --> 14:50.097
It seems certain
that they're growing upstream.

14:50.180 --> 14:52.683
There must be a ton of them!

14:56.020 --> 15:01.275
But there's no way that corn could drift
down the river just like that, right?

15:04.445 --> 15:07.239
Yes, Perseus here.

15:07.323 --> 15:10.117
<i>Hi! This is the search party!</i>

15:10.200 --> 15:12.453
<i>We found corn in the river.</i>

15:14.914 --> 15:18.042
It's not that we actually
found the plants.

15:18.125 --> 15:19.668
We just need a report.

15:19.752 --> 15:23.047
So, if you happen to find them
flowing down over there,

15:23.130 --> 15:25.549
-let us know.
<i>-Copy that!</i>

15:25.633 --> 15:27.635
Did you hear that?

15:27.718 --> 15:31.055
Pay attention and do not miss anything!

15:33.432 --> 15:34.725
That's great!

16:34.702 --> 16:35.744
It's around here…

16:35.828 --> 16:37.746
Like I said,

16:37.830 --> 16:39.957
there are many confusing place names.

16:40.040 --> 16:42.668
Do you mean, Sacramento City?

16:50.592 --> 16:52.845
What are you up to now, Senku?

16:54.638 --> 16:57.307
Why are you tying a white cloth
over the light?

17:00.769 --> 17:04.189
A light that bright at night
will attract insects.

17:04.273 --> 17:07.943
I am trying to attract insects
with this moth trap.

17:09.445 --> 17:12.364
By seeing what phototactic insects gather,

17:12.448 --> 17:14.408
you can tell what's in the area.

17:15.409 --> 17:17.536
If I understand correctly…

17:34.803 --> 17:37.556
The European corn borer

17:37.639 --> 17:39.683
is a moth that feeds on corn.

17:40.434 --> 17:43.437
There's a lot of corn nearby.

17:44.772 --> 17:47.399
So, butterflies? They're beautiful.

17:47.483 --> 17:48.859
No, they're moths.

17:48.942 --> 17:50.652
Butterflies or moths,

17:50.736 --> 17:52.863
those are just names
that people gave them.

17:54.073 --> 17:56.241
They have a mysterious kind of beauty.

17:58.577 --> 18:01.705
Kohaku… I mean, all of you, villagers.

18:02.289 --> 18:03.707
First of all,

18:03.791 --> 18:06.210
have you ever seen
this type of moth before?

18:08.462 --> 18:11.507
If you're asking me
whether I've seen it before,

18:11.590 --> 18:13.717
I'll say I've never
paid attention to moths.

18:13.801 --> 18:17.304
Moths feed on grains and fabric.

18:17.387 --> 18:20.808
In other words,
after humans got wiped out,

18:20.891 --> 18:23.477
a significant number of them
should have disappeared too.

18:24.353 --> 18:27.272
Still, I managed to attract this many

18:27.356 --> 18:29.399
in a short period of time.

18:29.483 --> 18:32.653
It's almost as if
there was a big corn farm around here.

18:34.363 --> 18:36.824
You're making such a serious face, Senku!

18:36.907 --> 18:38.784
We're going to revive
the whole of humanity.

18:38.867 --> 18:42.037
Isn't having a huge amount of corn
the best-case scenario?

18:51.255 --> 18:52.631
<i>What is this?</i>

19:07.646 --> 19:08.939
I feel a menacing aura…

19:09.773 --> 19:12.317
What is it? Is there some wild animal?

19:12.401 --> 19:15.487
Being such a powerful group,
we have nothing to worry about.

19:15.571 --> 19:17.531
You shouldn't say so while hiding.

19:17.614 --> 19:20.200
Do you recognize that aura?

19:20.284 --> 19:23.620
No, there's no such thing as an aura.

19:23.704 --> 19:26.206
I think it's just the pungent smell

19:26.290 --> 19:28.709
of adrenaline breakdown products.

19:28.792 --> 19:31.753
Though I'm not sure what it is myself…

19:33.213 --> 19:34.214
<i>Think…</i>

19:38.385 --> 19:40.721
<i>I have sworn to protect them.</i>

19:43.974 --> 19:46.810
<i>This faint scent blended in the air…</i>

19:51.023 --> 19:52.107
<i>Is it gunpowder?</i>

19:52.941 --> 19:54.902
<i>Do they have firearms?</i>

19:54.985 --> 19:59.156
This enemy is well prepared…

20:02.034 --> 20:04.369
It looks like we're the ones

20:05.078 --> 20:08.373
who fell into the moth trap…

20:15.088 --> 20:16.590
Back to the boats, everyone!

20:38.487 --> 20:39.321
It's a machine gun!

20:40.447 --> 20:41.740
What is going on?

20:46.161 --> 20:49.164
Don't underestimate me!
I'll steer this boat…

20:52.793 --> 20:54.378
to make a water barrier!

21:15.440 --> 21:17.526
Why did they find out?

21:21.321 --> 21:22.864
We're saved!

21:22.948 --> 21:24.950
Thanks to Tsukasa's quick reaction…

21:25.492 --> 21:27.369
No one is seriously injured, right?

21:27.452 --> 21:31.039
It can't be. Who would have a machine-gun?

21:31.123 --> 21:34.126
I thought there were absolutely no humans.

21:34.209 --> 21:37.045
Isn't America supposed to be safe?

21:37.129 --> 21:38.547
Not really.

21:38.630 --> 21:41.508
There's no explanation
for why the statues were abandoned.

21:42.175 --> 21:43.635
If there was any…

21:44.344 --> 21:47.264
Could it be that they have
a Revival Fluid similar to Senku's?

21:47.931 --> 21:49.224
What?

21:51.059 --> 21:53.061
Are they harvesting corn, too?

21:54.313 --> 21:55.647
In that case,

21:55.731 --> 21:58.358
I am extremely grateful.

22:00.569 --> 22:04.448
At worst, I could have had a real face-off
against a dark-side scientist.

22:06.491 --> 22:08.410
I can't afford to lose…

22:08.493 --> 22:09.953
That's all.

22:11.955 --> 22:14.499
They're pushing me, aren't they?

23:53.098 --> 23:55.934
NEXT EPISODE

23:56.017 --> 23:58.311
THIS WORK OF FICTION FEATURES ORGANISMS,
MATERIAL AND PROCESSES BASED ON REAL LIFE.

23:58.395 --> 24:00.897
COLLECTING OR REPLICATING THEM MAY BE
DANGEROUS AND ILLEGAL, SO DO NOT ATTEMPT.

24:00.981 --> 24:01.857
Subtitle translation by: Silvia
